FSHAND.C

sftprobe(): returns the slot number in sft of the first empty slot, or -1
if there aren't any.

syshnd(h): returns the sft slot number of h, indirecting through PD if nec.
Returns -NUMBIOS on error (i.e. handle out of range), and h if h itself is
negative (i.e. a BIOS device number, like Fwrite(-1,1,"x");).

xdup(h): h is std; uses a new slot in sft, copies the ofd field of the slot
that h refers to (after indirecting through PD).

xforce(std,h): call ixforce(std,h,run)

ixforce(std,h,pd): cause 'std' to refer to the same thing that 'h' does.
'h' is relative to run, but 'std' is relative to process 'pd'.  Calls
xclose(std) if std ultimately refers to a file.  Doesn't really need the
restriction that 'h' must not be a std handle, but has it for now.

xclose(h): for -ve handles, EIHNDL or a no-op.  For h referring to a file,
close the OFD and DFD if their use counts are 1.  Then, if h was a std,
re-open it on the default BIOS device.  Returns errors from closes before
doing side effects.  Calls dfd_close, ofd_close.  Does not yet flush all
buffers on all drives.

xread(handle, count, buffer): does the obvious.  Might get called with -ve
handle (calls char_read(h+3,len,buf)) or std handle referring to BIOS dev
(calls char_read(dev,len,buf)).  Otherwise shortens the request to at most
the rest of the file and calls xrw(0,ofd,len,buf,xfr2usr). Will not call either
(just returns zero) for zero length read on valid handle.

xwrite: same as ixread, but calls xrw(1,ofd,len,buf,usr2xfr).

xcreat(name,attr): calls ixcreat, but filters out illegal attr settings.
Then calls xopen to open the created file.

xgetdta, xsetdta, xsetdrv, xgetdrv

xsfirst(name,attr): calls ixsfirst(name,attr,run->p_xdta).

