FSFS.C

getdfd(dir): if there's a DFD referring to the file in 'dir' then return
a pointer to it, else NULL.

ixcreat(name,mode): create the file.  Just create it, don't open it.
Clobbers existing writable files by the same name, but doesn't clobber
files when creating directories, and doesn't clobber directories ever, and
refuses if the file is open or if the name is invalid.  Includes a special
case for FA_LABEL, which ISN'T CODED YET.

xopen(name,mode) does open stuff.  Calls getdfd to reuse an existing one,
or calls newdfd, fills it in and links in.  Calls newofd, fills it in,
links it to the dfd.  Links an sft slot to the ofd, returns that slot.
Initting the r/w pointer info isn't done yet.

xchmod(name,wflag,newmode): There are few restrictions on this call: you
CAN shoot yourself in the foot here.  You can't change the SUBDIR bit, and
you can't change the LABEL bit of a SUBDIR, but you can do anything else,
including changing wierd undefined bits.  Returns the old attrib for a
read, or the new for a write, or an error code.  The logic is that you want
access to the bits we haven't decided what to do with yet.

xdatime(buf,h,wflag): returns EIHNDL for invalid, negative, and TTY
handles.  Returns the date & time if !wflag, else writes the new info to
the DFD and marks the DFD as dirty.  Fsfirst has to check for the DFD or
its info will be out of date (which is also true of the length).

xdelete(name): external version of delete. Eventually calls ixdel to
actually remove the thing from the disk. Before doing that, checks to see
if somebody else has the file open. If so, returns EACCDN.  If *you* have
it open, calls xclose once per handle of yours that refers to the file.
NOTE: Fdelete of an open file on a write-protected disk will close the file
before failing to delete it.

ixdel(dir): removes a dir entry from the disk.  Just e5's out the slot and
frees the cluster chain. No sanity checks.  e5's out the slot first, so if
that write succeeds but others fail no harm is done.

xrename(oldname, newname): if the old name doesn't exist (as a file or
dir), error.  If the new path doesn't exist, error.  If the new file *does*
exist, error.  If the new name is invalid, error.  If the two aren't on the
same drive, error.  If old name is an open file, error.  If they're in the
same directory, copy the new name in. Else get FCB from old dir, mark as
erased & dirty, flush, copy FCB into new dir, copy new name over that, mark
new dir as dirty, flush.  Note that there is no check for renaming a
directory through the structure: this is as easy as renaming a file through
the structure.  It *will* change the CWD of anybody whose current directory
is the one being moved or a descendent.

xlseek(whither,h,whence): decodes 'whence' (with error checking) and places
the read-write pointer of h's OFD there.  Returns ERROR if any cluster
entry in the FAT has a value of zero or $FFFx, as these are illegal or
unexpected at this point.

