#define DIR struct _dir

DIR {
	int dir_dev;			/* dev this dir is on */
	unsigned int dir_clust;		/* start clust of this dir */
	unsigned int dir_recno;		/* record slot was found in */
	unsigned int dir_recoffs;	/* offs into recno of slot */
	BCB *dir_bcb;			/* buffer block (for marking dirty) */
	FCB *dir_fcb;			/* pointer to slot in cache buf */
};

errcode = fn2dir(pfn,dir,attr,dirflag): fill in *dir with directory
information while finding *pfn.  *pfn at the end contains a pointer to its
last component, or to the end of the string if there was a trailing SLASH
or dirflag is set.  Attr can limit the search.

The components of fn including colons and up to the last SLASH are consumed
and the result placed in the dir structure.  EFILNF and EPTHNF mean what
they say; in the former case, dev/clust are valid.  If the last component
to be scanned for is found, recno is the record number it was found in,
recoffs is the offset into that record, and buf points to the BCB and ptr
is a pointer to that slot in a cache buffer.  Great care must be taken not
to abuse buf or ptr, but they can be used to read or write info (see below).

Returns EACCDN when fn is empty, or the last component is empty and dirflag
is zero, or the last component starts with 0xe5 or dot-dot ("..") or
consists of just dot ("."), or contains wildcard characters.  This is
because these checks are so common.  (Need a flag saying dot and dot-dot
are OK!)

long dir2dir(dir) takes dev, recno and recoffs and fills in buf and ptr.
Again, these are valid only under special circumstances. ("Freshens" them.)

long dirslot(dir) takes a dev, clust and fills in recno, recoffs, buf and
ptr for the first slot in the directory which is erased (0xe5) or null
(0x00), and extends the directory if necessary.  Returns ERROR for full
disk and full root directory, zero for success.  If the directory needs
extending, this call does it and writes the FAT; if it fails, it does so in
a way that is not catastrophic, and returns an error code.

The basis of the dir system is that a dir structure is local to the routine
that uses it, and pointers to that are passed down to helpers. The bcb and
fcb fields are only usable when they're "fresh."  They're "fresh" when the
dir struct is filled by fn2dir with no error, and they become stale when
any call is made that might cause disk  activity. This includes read and
write, obviously, but also FAT operations, other calls to dir functions,
xclose, etc. It is NOT guaranteed that you can have two fresh dirs at a
time. The only routine which could want this is xrename, and it's coded so
as to avoid needing that.

