Oh, brother. We have to assume that a media-change error might occur in the
middle of any OS call, since even a read can cause a write to other drives
(to flush a dirty victim cache buffer), resulting in a "put disk B in drive
A..." and the user could put the wrong disk in.  Also, when we get back to
the read that started it all, the user could put the wrong disk *back* in
A, and we therefore get ANOTHER media change.  Furthermore, in the first
case, we could get a write error that's unrelated to the operation being
performed!  This complicates every call. Every call must be analyzed for
the results of media change at each step, in addition to read and write
errors.

Hmmm.. we can introduce the notion of "the drive the current call is acting
on."  There's only ever going to be one.  Only Frename *might* act on two
drives, and in that case it'll fail anyway.  So we treat errors differently
based on that: if it happens to the drive we're operating on, it's serious,
but if it happens to another drive, we get over it to some extent.

If you get E_CHNG you always start processing it the same way: close files,
invalidate buffers.  But if it happened on other than the current drive
(as can happen in writing a dirty victim) you then proceed with the
original call uninterrupted.  If it happened on the drive you're working
on, you longjmp to restart the call.

If you get another error you return the error code.  The caller then
cleans up modified data structures and returns the error.  The caller
should not cause any more I/O.  We declare a new error code which is
"I/O Error on another disk" to take care of that case.

Recovery from disk errors should NOT include invalidating cached buffers on
the drive, because that would be very bad.  What if you have unwritten data
and FAT sectors, then write-protect a disk, then get a WP error on a 
separate Fcreate and cancel, then un-write-protect it and close the
original file?  The close should succeed.  Hmm.  Should it?  This is pretty
far-fetched.  Just don't be stupid.

From POSIX (IEEE 1003.1) appendix B section B.2.2.1.2: 

	"Various exception conditions ... can cause data to be lost. ...
	The data can be lost, in whole or in part, so that only careful
	inspection of file contents could determine that an update did
	not occur. ... [This] can leave inconsistencies ... in the
	filesystem itself. ... The consequences of this to the user may
	be arbitrarily bad."

We already know that at the start of a GEMDOS call only open files can have
dirty data anywhere in GEMDOS memory: unwritten FAT and data blocks, and
un-updated directory entries.  Losing any of these things is not a
disaster, just annoying.  More to the point, the disk can't normally be
left in a state that would cause GEMDOS itself to bomb or get tied up in
knots. The worst case is still bad: two entries pointing at the same file
(half of an Frename). So you Fopen one of these, write into the first
sector (now it's cached), Fdelete the other one (the clusters are freed),
Dcreate a new directory (it gets those clusters), then write to the open
file again and close it.  Now the first sector of the directory is hosed. 
Sigh.  Half of a Drename is worse: which is the parent?

We could prevent this by checking all DFD's for the same starting cluster
number, to see if you're opening a file which itself has two entries, but
this is really pathological stuff.

fatflush(dm): write the FAT of drive DM out.  This is because we never want
the FAT to be out-of-date w.r.t. the disk after a GEMDOS call, except for
open files.

